      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3-3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)-3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
