    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3890",
      "knowledge_point_id": "67f41769-a0ff-5ecc-8b05-5890a7ac9fe3",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Using Arcsecant",
      "problem": "If $f'(x) = \\dfrac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2 - 1}} - 2x,$ then $f(x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm dx = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}-2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\text{arcsec}\\,(x)-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =5\\text{arcsec}\\,(x)-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
